 (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2(-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-\\frac{{e}^{2(0)}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042486/state.json" "file_sha256" "148f879e50920bbb60c00776e8408455ebe5df66683637a8801cf3ae2f787049"} {"question" "q-83012" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042486/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1bb557360bbee7325bccf0113aaf885633ba56b2616e357717414460c45b3b7b"} {"question" "q-83012" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042486/state.json" "file_sha256" "148f879e50920bbb60c00776e8408455ebe5df66683637a88